[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `tests/fresh_process.rs` | allocation-pattern equality across processes (1 test) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `scripts/check_features.sh` | every feature combination + release profile + parallel |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `scripts/mutation_check.sh` | proves the suite detects real bugs (23/23) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `scripts/check_c_optimization_levels.sh` | C at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 56 tests, green in every configuration, stable over 5 consecutive runs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The one thing that makes this library hard to test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `compare_allocations` compares the *addresses* of two live `malloc(4)` blocks:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int *ptr1 = malloc(sizeof(int));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int *ptr2 = malloc(sizeof(int));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (ptr1 < ptr2) result = 1; else if (ptr1 > ptr2) result = 2; else result = 3;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m uninit_ptr = ptr1;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m result += (*uninit_ptr > 0) ? 10 : 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Its return value is therefore **not a function of its arguments**. It is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m function of glibc's allocator state, and `arity4`/`arity3`/`arity2`/`arity` all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inherit that, because they add its result into theirs. Since glibc's tcache is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m LIFO free list, the address ordering *flips on every call*: the first call sees
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ascending addresses (`1`), the next sees descending (`2`), and so on.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Three consequences drove the whole test design:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **A naive differential harness reports a false failure.** Calling C once and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    then Rust once compares two *different* allocator states. Measured:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    interleaved calls give `(12, 11)` forever — and they give exactly the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `(12, 11)` when the C library is compared against **a second copy of itself**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `tests/methodology.rs::control_unseeded_interleaving_makes_c_disagree_with_itself`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    pins this down: the C library "disagrees with itself" on 10 out of 10 calls.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Any conclusion drawn from an unseeded 1:1 comparison is measuring glibc.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **So the tests force the allocator state instead.** `common::seed_heap`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    drains the 32-byte tcache bin and re-fills it so that the next `malloc` pair
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    is returned in a chosen order. It is called immediately before *every*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    measured call, on both sides. That makes both implementations fully
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    deterministic and — more usefully — lets every heap-sensitive row be tested
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    under **both** orderings, so the `result = 1` and `result = 2` branches are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    each reached deliberately rather than by luck.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    The draining is not incidental: glibc only pushes a freed chunk into the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    tcache while the bin is below capacity (7), and beyond that it goes to the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    fastbin. A seed that just does `free(hi); free(lo)` therefore stops
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    controlling anything once the bin happens to be full — which is exactly how
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `valid_arity_dispatch_len3` failed in the release profile, and only when run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    after other tests. `control_seeding_survives_any_prefilled_tcache` pre-loads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the bin with 0…12 chunks and checks the forced ordering still holds.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **What remains environment-dependent is stated, not hidden.** In a fresh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    process the first call's result depends on what the *dynamic loader* left in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the bin, and `dlopen` of a 4 MB Rust `cdylib` has a different allocation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    footprint than a 16 KB C `.so`. That is outside the library, and no faithful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    translation can control it — forcing a fixed answer in Rust would make it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    *diverge* from the C whenever the C returned the other one. What the Rust can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and does guarantee is an identical `malloc`/`free` pattern, which is verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    two ways:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    * `tests/fresh_process.rs` loads one implementation per process, seeds once,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      then runs a 500+-value script with no host allocations. The two processes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      produce identical sequences, so the number, size, order and placement of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      every allocation match.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    * A minimal external C driver (`dlopen` + `dlsym`, nothing else) gets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      identical output from both `.so`s, including the alternating pattern:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      `11 12 11 12 11 12` and `32 33 32 33 32 33`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Harness defects found (both would have produced a false "verified")
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **1. `cargo test` never built the `cdylib` — the suite tested a stale `.so`.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With `crate-type = ["cdylib"]` and integration tests that (correctly) do not link
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the crate, Cargo has no reason to produce `libarity_lib.so` at all. The loader
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m looked it up by path, found nothing in `target/debug/`, silently fell back to a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `target/release/` copy built before the tests existed, and reported 48 passing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests. Proof of how bad this was: `scripts/mutation_check.sh` injected 23
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deliberate behavioral bugs into `src/lib.rs` and the suite caught **0**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/common/mod.rs` now builds the `cdylib` on demand (separate target dir,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m profile matched to the test profile, features forwarded via
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `HARVEST_SO_FEATURES`) and asserts the artifact is newer than `src/lib.rs`. After
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the fix, **23 of 23** mutations are caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **2. The tcache seeding could be defeated by a full bin** (see point 2 above).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both are now guarded by tests, so neither can silently return.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors the C exhibits that the Rust had to reproduce
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each of these is a place a plausible translation would have gone wrong; all are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already correct in `src/lib.rs` and each is covered by a mutation that the suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m catches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`arity`'s parameter is `unsigned char`, not `int`.** The header says `int`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the definition says `unsigned char`, and the compiled code compares only the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   low byte with an *unsigned* branch. So `arity(256, p) == -1`, `arity(258, p)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   behaves like `arity(2, p)`, and `arity(-1, p)` is **not** rejected — it maps to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   255 and dispatches to `arity4`, reading four ints. Verified at every `-O` level.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`param1 % 4` can be negative.** C's `%` truncates toward zero, so a negative
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `param1` yields `-1`, `-2` or `-3` — none of which is a `switch` label in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `apply_bitmask`, so the mask is silently *not applied*. A translation using
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `rem_euclid` would look more "correct" and be wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`(result * param3) / 100` truncates toward zero,** not toward negative
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   infinity, so `div_euclid` is wrong for negative numerators.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Signed overflow wraps.** UB in C, but the emitted code wraps at every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   optimization level; the Rust uses explicit `wrapping_*` rather than panicking.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`shift_array`'s copy overlaps.** `memmove(arr + positions, arr, ...)` needs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ptr::copy`, not `copy_nonoverlapping`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`compare_allocations` reads through `uninit_ptr`, which aliases `ptr1`,**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so the `+10` bonus depends on `val1` and not on `val2`, and on `> 0` and not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `>= 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **No NULL checks anywhere.** The Rust adds none. Where a guard happens to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   short-circuit first (`arity` with `len < 2`, `shift_array` with a failing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   guard), a NULL pointer is safe in both and that is asserted; where it does not,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   both fault identically and there is no rejection value to compare.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`result = 3` and the `-1` OOM sentinel in `compare_allocations` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unreachable.** Two live allocations cannot share an address, and `malloc(4)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   does not fail. Rather than fake an allocation failure (which would test the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   interposer, not the library), the tests assert the observable consequence:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   neither `.so` ever returns those values across the whole randomized sweep.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reproducing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # C ground truth
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build \
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   && cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cargo test                                    # full suite (builds the .so itself)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ./scripts/check_features.sh                   # all feature combos + release + parallel
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ./scripts/mutation_check.sh                   # suite sensitivity: 23/23 caught
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ./scripts/check_c_optimization_levels.sh       # C at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Completion gate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` diff empty; 9/9 C symbols exported by Rust; 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       unresolvable non-libc imports. Asserted by `tests/symbols.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] Phase B: all 44 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       seed), each under both allocator orderings.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] Phase C: all 34 `ERRORS.md` rows have a passing error-path differential
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       test, asserting the same sentinel and not merely "both failed".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] Every configuration: default features, `--no-default-features` (the crate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       declares no `[features]`, so this is the complete powerset), the release
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       profile (`panic = "abort"` + optimizations), and parallel test threads.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] Suite sensitivity demonstrated: 23/23 injected bugs detected.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] Matches the C at `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3`, `-Os`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * [x] `src/lib.rs` unchanged from the reviewed translation (all mutations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       reverted; verified with `diff`).
